# PART 8 — DATABASE AND BACKEND ENFORCEMENT

## 8.1 Database Inspection

Before changes, identify:

- Authentication tables or provider.
- Organization records.
- Business records.
- Membership records.
- Role and permission records.
- Category records.
- Agent registry references, if implemented.
- Resource ownership fields.
- Existing database policies.
- Existing tenant isolation mechanisms.

## 8.2 Database Security

If GeFlow uses Supabase:

- Inspect existing Row Level Security policies.
- Ensure policies enforce organization and business boundaries.
- Avoid permissive policies that grant access to all authenticated users.
- Avoid conflicting or redundant policies.
- Protect server-only operations.
- Do not expose service-role credentials to frontend code.
- Validate authorization in privileged server-side functions.

If GeFlow uses Firebase or another backend, implement equivalent server-enforced access controls appropriate to the actual platform.

Do not introduce Supabase-specific implementation into a project that does not use Supabase.

## 8.3 API Authorization

Every affected API endpoint must validate:

- Authenticated identity.
- Organization membership.
- Business access.
- Category access.
- Required permission.
- Resource ownership.

Frontend checks are supplementary only.

Never rely exclusively on hidden buttons, disabled links or route guards.

## 8.4 Direct Database Access

Users and agents must not gain broader access by bypassing the application frontend.

Where direct client database access exists, enforce equivalent security policies at the database layer.

---

# PART 9 — FRONTEND ACCESS CONTROL

## 9.1 UI Permission Behavior

The frontend must display only authorized organization and category options.

Examples:

- Unauthorized businesses should not appear in the user's business selector.
- Unauthorized category tools should not appear as usable options.
- Restricted navigation items should follow existing UI conventions.
- Access-denied states should be clear.
- Suspended membership should not retain normal access.

## 9.2 Backend Remains Authoritative

Frontend visibility is not authorization.

A user must not gain access by:

- Editing browser state.
- Changing URL parameters.
- Calling APIs manually.
- Replaying requests.
- Modifying category IDs.
- Modifying organization IDs.
- Modifying agent IDs.

## 9.3 Existing UI Preservation

Do not redesign the dashboard, navigation or organization interface.

Implement only the minimum UI changes required to reflect actual authorization outcomes.

If no UI change is required, do not change the UI.

